1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,812
Aarav reads a line in his lab manual.

2
00:00:02,812 --> 00:00:04,542
Current gain, one hundred.

3
00:00:04,842 --> 00:00:08,283
Can one small part really make
current a hundred times bigger?

4
00:00:08,583 --> 00:00:10,470
And what do alpha and beta mean?

5
00:00:10,970 --> 00:00:14,795
A transistor has three legs:
emitter, base and collector.

6
00:00:15,095 --> 00:00:18,195
But a circuit needs two
wires in and two wires out.

7
00:00:18,495 --> 00:00:21,726
So one leg must be shared
by the input and the output.

8
00:00:22,026 --> 00:00:24,314
Share the base, and it is common base.

9
00:00:24,314 --> 00:00:26,451
Share the collector, common collector.

10
00:00:26,801 --> 00:00:29,446
Share the emitter, and
it is common emitter.

11
00:00:29,446 --> 00:00:31,114
This is the one used most.

12
00:00:31,714 --> 00:00:34,955
Let's build the common emitter
setup on the breadboard.

13
00:00:35,255 --> 00:00:38,147
The input goes between
the base and the emitter.

14
00:00:38,497 --> 00:00:41,267
The output comes from the
collector and the emitter.

15
00:00:41,617 --> 00:00:43,782
The emitter is shared by both sides.

16
00:00:43,782 --> 00:00:45,772
That is why it is called common.

17
00:00:46,122 --> 00:00:48,741
A small battery forward biases the base.

18
00:00:48,741 --> 00:00:51,153
A bigger battery feeds the collector.

19
00:00:51,503 --> 00:00:53,442
The input current is IB.

20
00:00:53,442 --> 00:00:55,433
The output current is IC.

21
00:00:56,033 --> 00:00:59,162
Now picture one hundred
runners leaving the emitter.

22
00:00:59,462 --> 00:01:01,687
Ninety nine of them reach the collector.

23
00:01:02,037 --> 00:01:03,704
Only one gets stuck in the base.

24
00:01:04,304 --> 00:01:07,725
Alpha asks: what fraction of
runners reached the collector?

25
00:01:08,075 --> 00:01:10,227
Ninety nine out of one hundred.

26
00:01:10,227 --> 00:01:12,330
Alpha is zero point nine nine.

27
00:01:12,930 --> 00:01:17,080
Beta asks: how many reach the collector
for each one stuck in the base?

28
00:01:17,430 --> 00:01:19,285
Ninety nine for every one.

29
00:01:19,285 --> 00:01:20,779
Beta is ninety nine.

30
00:01:21,379 --> 00:01:24,329
So alpha is always a bit less than one.

31
00:01:24,329 --> 00:01:25,438
Beta is big.

32
00:01:26,037 --> 00:01:27,132
Now the formulas.

33
00:01:27,132 --> 00:01:29,124
Alpha equals IC divided by IE.

34
00:01:29,474 --> 00:01:32,353
It is the current gain
of the common base setup.

35
00:01:33,053 --> 00:01:35,415
Beta equals IC divided by IB.

36
00:01:35,415 --> 00:01:38,077
It is the common emitter current gain.

37
00:01:38,777 --> 00:01:40,716
Start from the current rule.

38
00:01:40,716 --> 00:01:42,634
IE equals IB plus IC.

39
00:01:43,334 --> 00:01:45,349
Divide every term by IC.

40
00:01:46,049 --> 00:01:49,933
One over alpha equals
one over beta, plus one.

41
00:01:50,633 --> 00:01:51,653
Rearrange it.

42
00:01:51,653 --> 00:01:54,175
Alpha equals beta over one plus beta.

43
00:01:54,875 --> 00:01:57,932
Or beta equals alpha over one minus alpha.

44
00:01:58,632 --> 00:02:00,938
Small changes work the same way.

45
00:02:00,938 --> 00:02:04,361
AC beta is change in IC over change in IB.

46
00:02:04,961 --> 00:02:06,781
Now the breadboard graphs.

47
00:02:06,781 --> 00:02:08,346
First the input side.

48
00:02:08,697 --> 00:02:10,298
Raise VBE slowly.

49
00:02:10,298 --> 00:02:12,487
Almost nothing flows at first.

50
00:02:12,837 --> 00:02:16,990
Past about zero point seven
volts, the base current shoots up.

51
00:02:16,990 --> 00:02:18,395
Just like a diode.

52
00:02:18,995 --> 00:02:20,552
Now the output side.

53
00:02:20,552 --> 00:02:22,998
Keep IB fixed and raise VCE.

54
00:02:23,348 --> 00:02:26,137
IC climbs quickly, then goes almost flat.

55
00:02:26,487 --> 00:02:28,733
The flat part is the active region.

56
00:02:28,733 --> 00:02:30,998
Here IC hardly depends on VCE.

57
00:02:31,348 --> 00:02:34,982
Give a bigger IB, and the
flat line sits higher.

58
00:02:34,982 --> 00:02:36,379
Beta times bigger.

59
00:02:36,979 --> 00:02:38,864
Alpha is just below one.

60
00:02:38,864 --> 00:02:39,974
Beta is big.

61
00:02:39,974 --> 00:02:43,077
And beta equals alpha
over one minus alpha.

62
00:02:43,877 --> 00:02:44,924
Worked example.

63
00:02:44,924 --> 00:02:48,241
A transistor has alpha equal
to zero point nine eight.

64
00:02:48,591 --> 00:02:49,682
Find beta.

65
00:02:50,032 --> 00:02:52,924
Beta equals alpha over one minus alpha.

66
00:02:53,624 --> 00:02:56,823
That is zero point nine eight
over zero point zero two.

67
00:02:57,523 --> 00:02:59,458
Beta equals forty nine.

68
00:02:59,458 --> 00:03:02,217
So IC is forty nine times IB.

69
00:03:02,867 --> 00:03:04,331
Now, the exam trap.

70
00:03:04,681 --> 00:03:07,000
Many students think alpha
can be more than one.

71
00:03:07,350 --> 00:03:08,250
It cannot.

72
00:03:08,183 --> 00:03:12,401
IC is always a little less than
IE, so alpha stays below one.

73
00:03:13,051 --> 00:03:14,997
And never mix them up.

74
00:03:14,997 --> 00:03:16,460
Alpha uses IE.

75
00:03:16,460 --> 00:03:17,863
Beta uses IB.

76
00:03:18,513 --> 00:03:19,656
Where is it asked?

77
00:03:19,656 --> 00:03:22,502
PUC and ISC board papers
ask for these relations.

78
00:03:22,852 --> 00:03:26,733
Expect numericals on alpha and
beta, and the characteristic curves.

79
00:03:27,383 --> 00:03:28,283
Try this.

80
00:03:28,131 --> 00:03:32,024
A transistor in common emitter
mode has beta equal to ninety nine.

81
00:03:32,024 --> 00:03:34,312
The emitter current is five milliamps.

82
00:03:34,312 --> 00:03:35,954
What is the base current?

83
00:03:41,354 --> 00:03:42,254
B.

84
00:03:41,638 --> 00:03:42,538
Fifty microamps.

85
00:03:42,799 --> 00:03:46,098
First, alpha equals ninety
nine over one hundred.

86
00:03:46,098 --> 00:03:48,169
That is zero point nine nine.

87
00:03:48,519 --> 00:03:51,554
So IC equals zero point
nine nine times five.

88
00:03:51,554 --> 00:03:53,577
Four point nine five milliamps.

89
00:03:53,927 --> 00:03:56,739
IB equals five minus four point nine five.

90
00:03:56,739 --> 00:03:58,356
That is fifty microamps.

91
00:03:59,006 --> 00:04:00,552
Screenshot this card.

92
00:04:03,752 --> 00:04:06,874
Next, the transistor as a
switch and an amplifier.

93
00:04:06,874 --> 00:04:09,444
How does it turn a whisper into a shout?
